    port. The `--live` smoke gains one full agent turn (tools ride along
    only when the model advertises them — the backend hard-rejects tools
    for models without support).

Version: 1.56

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Phase 6: operational hardening and configuration integration for
    `ollamarun serve`. Every `serve_*` key is now validated at startup by
    one matrix (`helpers.serve_settings`): types, ranges and zero
    semantics are checked (a configured 0 keeps its documented meaning —
    `*_timeout` disables, `*_ttl` keeps forever, the artifact caps mean
    unlimited), and the transport values actually reach the server —
    `serve_body_limit`, `serve_request_timeout` (per-operation) and
    `serve_sse_ping_interval` are wired into `HttpServer` instead of the
    hard-coded defaults.
  - Chunked request bodies decode properly: the cumulative decoded size
    is capped at `serve_body_limit` before each chunk is read, chunk
    extensions are ignored, trailers are drained bounded, and malformed
    framing (bad sizes, missing CRLF, truncation, a missing terminal
    chunk, `Transfer-Encoding` together with `Content-Length`, a
    non-chunked coding) answers a structured 400 instead of the old 411.
  - Aggregate input limits bound what a request *resolves to*, not just
    what it sends: `serve_max_messages`, `serve_max_content_parts`,
    `serve_max_message_bytes`, `serve_max_text_bytes`, `serve_max_images`
    and `serve_max_image_bytes` are charged across both API surfaces by
    one budget (counts fail before any fetch; violations are 413
    `input_too_large` naming the limit).
  - Native `settings.parameters` go through one validator: known Ollama
    options are type/range checked (matching the OpenAI mapper where they
    overlap), unknown names pass through bounded (scalars/lists of
    scalars, string/list/object size caps), non-finite numbers and nested
    structures are refused. `PATCH /v1/sessions/{id}` gains a validated
    `parameters` field (merged into the session).
  - The API tool context is jailed to `serve_workspace` (default: the
    startup cwd): path-argument tools resolve relative paths under it and
    refuse resolved escapes, symlinks included; `run_shell`/`run_python`
    working directories follow the rule. It is an argument/default-cwd
    policy, never subprocess confinement, and the CLI is unchanged. The
    artifact store asserts the same resolved-root containment on every
    index/manifest path it reads back.
  - `serve_models` is one API-wide tag-aware allowlist (literal or
    fnmatch patterns; empty = every model) applied to the model list,
    lookup, chat, sessions, turns (stored sessions included) and fits —
    a forbidden model answers the same 404 `model_not_found` as an
    unknown one. `serve_agent: false` serves models, chat and the shared
    file/artifact endpoints while the native agent surface answers 404
    (`add_agent_routes` split into `add_artifact_routes` +
    `add_agent_routes` over one `build_agent_api`).
  - Shutdown is an ordered, bounded lifecycle: stop accepting, refuse new
    background work (`begin_shutdown`, racing handlers answer 503),
    resolve parked approvals as cancelled, cancel/join turns and fits,
    drain in-flight HTTP handlers within `serve_shutdown_timeout`, then
    close. `serve_unload_on_exit` (default true) unloads only the models
    this server actually used — embedding models stay resident and a
    parallel CLI session's model is never evicted; `--keep-loaded` turns
    it off. The banner prints the workspace root and the surface in
    effect.

Version: 1.55

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Context and placement control over the native API. A session's context
    size and GPU placement are first-class fields: `PATCH /v1/sessions/{id}`
    and the agent-turn `settings` accept `num_ctx` (a positive integer,
    refused past the model's trained maximum) and `gpu_layers` (the CLI
    spec grammar, resolved against `/api/show` at validation time); a
    placement change unloads the resident model, `/gpu-layers` parity.
  - Fitting comes in two speeds. `POST /v1/agent/fit` is the synchronous
    estimate (no model loads; `mode` gpu/cpu, optional `gpu_layers` pin and
    `apply`), returning the chosen `num_ctx` and the whole fit report as
    data. `POST /v1/agent/fits` starts the same fit as an asynchronous
    probe job that verifies the boundary by loading models: 202 with the
    job id and honest warnings (resident models / active turns make it
    slower; prior measurements on the host seed the probe), `GET
    /v1/agent/fits/{id}` for status/progress/result, `POST .../cancel` to
    stop it. A job with a session holds it — turns, edits and a second fit
    get 409 `session_busy` with `active_fit_id` — the global cap answers
    429, and terminal jobs live until `serve_fit_ttl`. `serve_fit` gates
    the surface: "probe" (default), "estimate" or "off".
  - Turn-start fitting: `settings.num_ctx` also accepts `"max-gpu"` /
    `"max-cpu"` (with `settings.cpu_percent`). The estimate runs at turn
    start (never a blocking probe), the fitted context is persisted and
    reported as the new typed `session.fit` event plus `result.fit` on the
    turn status; a failed fit leaves the context unchanged and reports the
    reason on the same event. `ctxfit`'s compute path now returns a
    structured `FitOutcome` (the CLI's reports print from it,
    behavior unchanged) and honours cancellation/progress checkpoints.
  - The OpenAI-compatible surface deliberately gains none of this: it
    stays a stateless translation proxy (no session to configure; fitting
    per request would mean load churn per completion). Locked.

Version: 1.54

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The Phase 5 artifact layer. `ollamarun serve` now has one canonical
    artifact model behind `POST /v1/files` (a strict JSON upload,
    `{filename, media_type, data_base64}` -> 201 with the full
    `ArtifactRef`: opaque `art_…` id, media type, sanitized display
    filename, size, sha256, source, turn/session provenance, created_at
    and the relative `url`), `GET /v1/artifacts/{id}` (the stored bytes
    with their media type, an ASCII-safe Content-Disposition and
    nosniff) and `GET /v1/artifacts/{id}/meta`. Contents are immutable
    snapshots (a later edit of the original file never changes a stored
    artifact), lookups go through per-artifact manifests (a validated id
    never builds a storage path), and the store enforces one per-file
    cap (`serve_artifact_max_bytes`, default 16 MiB under the 32 MiB
    body cap), a locked quota (`serve_artifact_quota_bytes`, 2 GiB;
    concurrent uploads cannot overshoot) and TTL retention
    (`serve_artifact_ttl`, 7 days; startup + opportunistic sweeps also
    reap stale reservations and debris).
  - Messages meet files through one shared resolver, so the OpenAI
    surface and native agent turns behave identically: a
    `{"type": "file", "file_id": "art_…"}` part inlines text-ish files
    as a bounded `<file name="…">` block (32 KiB, truncation noted),
    sends image files through the same vision capability check as
    `image_url` parts, describes unsupported binaries as downloadable
    but not processed, and answers a missing/expired id with a
    structured 404 before inference. Agent turns now accept
    string-or-parts content; `image_url` accepts http(s) URLs through
    one bounded fetch (scheme allowlist, 3 redirects, timeouts, 10 MiB
    cap; the SSRF caveat is the locked trusted-network boundary).
  - Tools publish artifacts. `ToolHandlerResult(text, artifact_paths)`
    lets `write_file`/`edit_file`/`copy_file`/`move_file` snapshot their
    result (regular files only; `copy_file`/`move_file` publish the
    destination) and `run_python`/`run_shell` outputs go through the new
    approval-gated `publish_artifact` tool (cwd-jailed, size-capped,
    present only when serve runs a store — the CLI is unchanged). Each
    publication emits `artifact.created` (the full ref) between the
    approval decision and `tool.result`, which references the ids;
    `TurnResult.artifacts`, the turn status body, the session's artifact
    list and `GET /v1/sessions/{id}` carry the refs, while message
    payloads never leak them to the model. `persist: false` artifacts
    are still stored and served, and deleting a session leaves its
    artifacts to their own TTL.
  - Housekeeping: `serve_approval_timeout: 0` now really disables the
    approval timeout (it used to fall back to 300 s), with a regression
    test.

Version: 1.53

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Pre-Phase-5 housekeeping. `parallel_tool_calls` is type-checked like
    every other field the OpenAI surface accepts (a boolean or absent;
    anything else is a structured 400) while its semantics stay with the
    compatibility phase. A parked approval cancelled with its turn now
    reports why — "cancelled (turn timeout)", "cancelled (client
    disconnected)", "cancelled (server shutting down)" or "cancelled by
    user" — instead of always "cancelled by user", and the documented
    `Last-Event-ID` resume path gained its regression test (a malformed
    header answers 400). Stale "next phase"/"Phase N" references in the
    runtime and API comments were cleared up, and the README now states
    that an events subscription without `?after=`/`Last-Event-ID` is
    live-only (a finished turn yields an empty stream).

Version: 1.52

Summary of changes in this version:
  - `/num-ctx --max-gpu` and `--max-cpu` now size their first-principles
    estimate from a real model profile: `general.parameter_count` and the
    on-disk size are cross-checked against the quantization
    (`details.quantization_level`/`general.file_type`), per-layer KV geometry
    covers hybrid attention, sliding windows (their cache saturates at
    `window + batch`), per-layer SWA key/value dims and MLA's compressed
    latent plus rope tail, recurrent `ssm.*`/shortconv state is sized
    separately, and a pinned `/gpu-layers` counts the output layer and the
    topmost blocks instead of an even weight split. The fit report names the
    model's params, quantization, architecture and weights source.
  - The measured-context cache is still there as a refinement: the current
    resident model and prior probes seed the bytes-per-token estimate and the
    probe search, while the profile formula is the fallback and floor. The
    bounded, host/model/placement-scoped cache lives at
    `~/etc/data/ollamarun/ctxfit-calibration.json`. The GPU estimate no longer
    subtracts a second 256 MiB buffer reserve (`max_gpu_overhead_mb` now
    defaults to 0): llama.cpp's own fit projection and `max_gpu_margin_mb`
    already cover those buffers, so the estimate lands close to the real
    boundary (granite4.2:3b 6,144 -> 9,216 against a measured 10,240 here).
    Probe candidates halve when nothing fits and step just below a partial
    load, and the probe caps default to 10.

Version: 1.51

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Tool names that leak a call id or namespace into the function name
    (`call_fetch_url`, `tool_curl`, `functions.web_search`) now resolve
    onto the real tool: `resolve_tool_alias()` retries an unknown name once
    with the prefix stripped, against both the alias map and the canonical
    definition names, so the call runs instead of coming back as "unknown
    or disabled tool" (issue #26). The default tool rules also state the
    contract that a tool result is data, not a user turn: after a failed or
    unrun call the model is told to fix the call or explain the failure,
    never to reply to the tool output as if the user had written it.

Version: 1.50

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The Phase 4 review fixes. Turn retention is enforced by the events
    endpoint itself: `TurnManager.subscribe()` sweeps expired records like
    `get()`/`submit()` do, so a completed turn past `serve_turn_event_ttl`
    answers 410 instead of serving a stale replay (it used to depend on
    something else having swept first). The manager's synthesized error
    terminals (a crashed work closure, or a result returned without one)
    carry the stable "the turn failed internally" message while the raw
    exception text goes to the `--debug` log and stays on the internal
    result, so the event stream no longer leaks paths the status endpoint
    was already hiding. And `PATCH /v1/sessions/{id}` is refused with the
    same 409 `session_busy` as DELETE while the session has an active turn,
    closing a concurrent-mutation window against the worker's saves.
    Regression tests cover the direct-subscribe 410, the sanitized crash
    stream and the PATCH guard.

Version: 1.49

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The native agent API landed (`ollamarun_lib/agent_api.py`): a client
    starts a full agent turn (`POST /v1/agent/turns`, 202) and follows it
    over SSE (`GET /v1/agent/turns/{id}/events`; typed events with
    `<turn>:<seq>` ids, `?after=`/`Last-Event-ID` replay, `stream.gap`
    passthrough), checks its snapshot (404 unknown / 410 swept), cancels
    it explicitly and browses or edits the persistent sessions
    (`/v1/sessions` CRUD). Turns run through the existing
    `TurnManager`/`AgentRuntime` with a headless `Repl`, so the CLI's
    policy engine applies unchanged: `approval: "policy"` (the default)
    runs allowlisted/rule-covered calls and denies the rest with a note,
    while `approval: "interactive"` parks undecided calls in
    `waiting_approval` — `approval.required` carries an `approval_id` for
    `POST /v1/agent/approvals/{id}` (run/remember/rule/auto/deny; the
    first decision wins, duplicates are 409, a timeout denies with
    "approval timed out"). A disconnect only unsubscribes (unless
    `cancel_on_disconnect`), while cancel/shutdown abort the in-flight
    generation, kill cancellable subprocesses and resolve waiting
    approvals as cancelled. One active turn per session (409
    `session_busy` with the active id), a global cap (429) and an
    optional `serve_turn_timeout` (reason `timeout`). Also in this
    version: `policy_check` (the shared non-interactive half of the
    approval gate, now used by both the CLI and the API), the headless
    context warning/auto-compaction at turn start (closing Phase 1b item
    13 through the runtime's `on_started` hook), `CancelToken.reason` so
    a timeout is distinguishable from a user stop, and turn snapshots
    with OpenAI-shaped usage plus the detailed stats. Full suite green;
    live-checked against Ollama 0.40 (policy-mode turn, interactive
    park/resolve, event replay, session CRUD and cleanup).

Version: 1.48

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The Phase 3 review fixes landed. A malformed first message plus the
    `system` extension is a structured 400 now (it used to raise through
    to a 500); upstream failures no longer leak backend detail — clients
    see stable `model_not_found`/`backend_unreachable`/`backend_error`
    messages while the raw text goes to the `--debug` log; and the
    `ollamarun serve` socket timeout is actually wired
    (`HttpServer(request_timeout=…)` was dead): a stalled body gets a 408
    while a stream that outlives the timeout still completes — it is a
    per-operation timeout, not a deadline.
  - Request validation went strict: `n` must be the integer 1,
    `stream_options.include_usage` a JSON boolean, `tool_choice` requires
    tools, unknown keys inside tools/`tool_choice`/`response_format`
    wrappers are rejected, `strict: true` tool schemas are rejected as
    unsupported (`false` is a no-op) and the `json_schema` wrapper is
    validated (only its `schema` is forwarded). One image content part is
    capped at 10 MiB decoded (`413 image_too_large`), and the model-list
    and capability caches are single-flight, so concurrent cold requests
    share one upstream fetch.
  - Tests grew the malformed-request matrix with exact envelopes,
    real-socket lifecycle coverage (stalled body 408, streams longer than
    the timeout, shutdown mid-stream, two-stream disconnect isolation),
    cache concurrency, SDK negative paths plus a real mid-stream
    `openai.APIError`, and an opt-in `--live` real-backend smoke test.
    Every remaining compatibility item moved into the plans: chunked
    request decoding (phase 6) and `plans/phase-9-compatibility.md`
    (embeddings, `n>1`, `parallel_tool_calls`, remote image URLs and the
    recorded closures for backend-impossible features).

Version: 1.47

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The API layer's first HTTP phase landed: `ollamarun serve` runs an
    OpenAI-compatible API in front of Ollama (`GET /v1/models`,
    `GET /v1/models/{id}`, `POST /v1/chat/completions`, streaming or not),
    so standard OpenAI clients work against the local models with a custom
    base URL. Two new modules: `server.py` (generic threading-HTTP
    plumbing: router, body caps checked before parsing, request timeouts,
    sanitized error envelopes, an SSE writer with a `: ping` heartbeat and
    a disconnect callback, SIGINT/SIGTERM shutdown) and `openai_api.py`
    (the `/v1` mapping). Model turns are a stateless translation proxy:
    one inference call, client `tools` passed through and returned as
    standard `tool_calls` — never executed — no session writes, and
    thinking models surface as `delta.reasoning_content`.
    Compatibility gaps fail explicitly (n>1, logprobs, logit_bias, audio,
    non-text modalities, unknown fields, `tool_choice: "required"`, remote
    image URLs for now) and images are validated against the model's
    vision capability; the request extension `system: "auto"` sends the
    built-in default prompt. `--bind`/`--port` (default 0.0.0.0:11440,
    LAN-accessible without authentication, trusted-network-only) stay
    separate from the upstream `--host`/`--remote`, and a disconnecting
    streaming client cancels only its own upstream generation. Phase 1b
    item 12 closed by deviation: model turns live in the OpenAI layer, not
    as an `AgentRuntime` mode, so the dead `mode` fields were removed.
    Tests cover the mapping, the live HTTP surface over a fake backend,
    client-disconnect cancellation, the serve CLI, and a real OpenAI SDK
    round-trip (skipped when the package is absent).

Version: 1.46

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The turn manager arrived (`ollamarun_lib/turns.py`). Every runtime
    event now gets a deterministic envelope (`TurnEvent`) carrying the
    turn id, a 1-based per-turn `seq` and an ISO timestamp, and
    `TurnManager` runs each turn on its own worker thread: one active
    turn per session, a global cap, cancellation through the turn's
    `CancelToken`, and typed errors (`SessionBusy`, `TooManyTurns`,
    `UnknownTurn`, `TurnExpired`) for the HTTP layer to map later.
    Events are buffered per turn and replayed to reconnecting
    subscribers (`after=<seq>`); when the requested window predates the
    buffer, or a subscriber falls too far behind, the first thing it
    sees is a `stream.gap` telling it to re-read the turn/session state
    instead of assuming continuity. Completed turns are retained for a
    day and then answer "expired"; a subscriber that cannot keep up is
    closed, so a missing reader can never stall a turn. The runtime is
    untouched — the manager wraps it — and a headless Repl turn already
    runs through it end to end. The serve-phase keys
    (`serve_max_active_turns`, `serve_turn_event_buffer`,
    `serve_turn_event_ttl`) are in place.

Version: 1.45

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Fix: stopping a turn really kills the tool that is running now. The
    Phase 1b helpers were correct, but `Repl` built its `ToolContext`
    before the `CancelToken` existed and never handed the token over, so
    every handler ran with `cancel=None` in a real turn: Esc during a
    `run_shell` waited for the command, kept its output and recorded it
    as a success. One assignment wires it; an end-to-end regression test
    fails without it.
  - The Phase 1 audit tests that found it ride along and lock the rest
    down: the event vocabulary, JSON-ready payloads and single-terminal
    contract (error and cancellation included), the approval order on
    the allowed/denied/interrupted/stopped paths, an unattended turn
    refusing a risky call without ever parking, mid-turn `/render raw`
    silencing that round's tool event panels, the `<index>.lock` flock
    blocking another process (with the no-`fcntl` fallback), two writers
    of one session keeping a valid file with no temp leftovers, and
    `delete_file` refusing to trash anything after a stop.


Version: 1.44

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The Phase 1 runtime grew its remaining rails. The tool-support
    capability check belongs to `AgentRuntime` now: it runs after
    `turn.started`, warns once per model (through the host's seen set),
    and a headless front end receives it as a `turn.warning` event.
    `quiet_tools` is read live, so `/render raw` during a turn silences
    the tool panels from the next event on. Stopping a turn now reaches
    every subprocess-backed tool — the ssh probes behind `system_status`,
    the chemInventoryAPI runner, tts-remote say/voices and the `trash-put`
    behind `delete_file` — and a call attempted after the stop refuses to
    spawn its child at all (`run_cancellable()`/`CommandCancelled` now
    live in `helpers.py` so the probes share them).
  - `@file` mentions actually inline: both the REPL and the one-shot path
    stored the raw prompt and threw the expanded text away, so the model
    only ever saw the bare path; the stored (and sent) message now carries
    the `<file>` blocks, while the bubble keeps what the user typed.
  - The session index is safe across processes: `SessionStore` takes an
    exclusive `flock` on `<index>.lock` around its read-modify-write (on
    top of the shared in-process lock), and session/index temp files get a
    per-writer name, so a CLI saving while serve runs cannot drop an
    entry. A Python 3.10 syntax guard and `TurnRequest`/`TurnResult`
    mapping tests close out the phase.

Version: 1.43

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The agent turn loop moved out of `repl.py` into a new `runtime.py`
    (`AgentRuntime`) driven by typed events (`events.py`): the CLI keeps
    `_generate`/`_execute_tool_calls`/`_payload`/`context_report`/`session_report`
    as thin wrappers and renders exactly as before, while a host can now run
    a turn with no terminal at all. A `HeadlessRenderer` consumes the same
    streams and emits `message.delta` events instead of printing, so the
    planned serve front end shares the one loop rather than duplicating it
    (`run.py --json` already does). The preliminary events vocabulary is
    `turn.started`, `message.delta`, `message.completed`, `tool.call`,
    `tool.result`, `approval.required`, `approval.resolved`, `turn.error`,
    `turn.completed`, `turn.cancelled` (plus `turn.warning`), with
    `artifact.created` reserved for the artifact phase.
  - Stopping a turn now reaches the tools: `run_shell`/`run_python` run in
    their own process group and are killed on cancel, network tools
    (`fetch_url`, `web_search`) are abandoned — the result is discarded
    instead of the stop waiting for a slow page — and the runtime marks the
    cancelled calls explicitly. The approval gate asks the front end
    (`Ui.interactive`) rather than reading the terminal, sessions save under
    a store-wide lock (safe for the parallel turns serve will run), `send()`
    owns the cancel-token reset, and a headless turn no longer inlines
    `@file` mentions or spends an extra call on auto titles.

Version: 1.42

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The tool harness is fully per-tool controllable from the prompt. `/tools`
    now takes a tool name (or one of its invented aliases) as its first
    token: `/tools web_search off` toggles just that tool, `/tools web_search
    help` prints a page generated from the tool's definition — description,
    every parameter with its type and whether it is required or optional,
    aliases, approval behaviour and, when the tool is not installed, what it
    needs (ddgs, tts-remote, chemInventoryAPI) — and `/tools web_search call
    ARGS` runs the tool yourself through the same approval gate and result
    panel as a model call (JSON, `key=value` pairs, or a bare value when a
    single parameter is required); a manual call never touches the
    conversation. `/tools help` (and `/help tools`) is the reference. Tab
    completion walks the whole command in both front ends: subcommands and
    tool names after `/tools`, aliases once a prefix is typed, per-tool
    actions after a name, and the keywords after enable/disable, policy and
    rules add.

Version: 1.41

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Textual really is optional now. `tui.py` imports the Textual classes
    at module level and `ollamarun_lib/__init__.py` re-exported it
    unconditionally, so a machine without the `textual` package could not
    run any command (`--version` included) even though the inline REPL is
    the documented fallback. The re-export is now conditional on
    `HAS_TEXTUAL`; `--ui tui` keeps printing its install hint and falls
    back as before, and a regression test runs the CLI with the textual
    import blocked.

Version: 1.40

Summary of changes in this version:
  - A missing `prompt_toolkit` no longer takes the whole CLI down before
    it starts: the fallback in `ollamarun_lib/constants.py` now binds
    subclassable stub classes instead of `None`, so `repl.py`'s
    module-level `SlashCompleter(Completer)` definition can no longer
    crash the import with `TypeError: NoneType takes no arguments` (with
    the dependency absent every command died, `--version` included).
    Without prompt_toolkit the inline REPL degrades to plain `input()`
    prompts, which is what the `HAS_PROMPT_TOOLKIT` checks always
    intended; a regression test now imports the package and runs
    `--version` in subprocesses with the prompt_toolkit import blocked.

Version: 1.39

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The model can see its own environment. `context_status` reports the
    context budget: the window actually in use (a session `num_ctx`, else
    the loaded runner's `/api/ps` window, else the model default — labeled
    which), the tokens the last request carried, the free space and the
    message counts, with a wrap-up note past 80% full. `session_info`
    reports the current date and time, the session id and title, the model
    with its size, quantization and capabilities, the server and working
    directory, the active parameters, the thinking/render modes, the
    system-prompt status, and the tool policy with the round budget. Both
    are read-only.

Version: 1.38

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The context counter and the context warnings measure against the window
    the model is actually loaded with, not the model's architectural
    maximum: the footer's denominator is now `/api/ps`'s `context_length`
    when a runner is loaded (a qwen3.5 loaded at 65536 reads
    `ctx 8.012k/65.536k (12.225%)` instead of `.../262.144k (3.056%)`), a
    session `num_ctx` still wins (the next request asks for it), and a
    pre-load answer is re-checked once the runner shows up. `/context` and
    the 80%/85% usage warnings use the same window.

Version: 1.37

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The model can edit files instead of only rewriting them whole.
    `edit_file` replaces one exact text fragment (unique match unless
    `replace_all` is set), writes atomically and keeps the file mode; the
    call panel shows a coloured diff before the edit is approved. The rest
    of the file chores get small tools too: `make_dir` (mkdir -p),
    `move_file`/`copy_file` (refuse an existing destination unless
    `overwrite=true`) and `delete_file` (trash-put only, never a permanent
    unlink). Path approval rules cover edit_file/make_dir/delete_file.
  - The model can look things up without shelling out: `search_sessions`
    searches the user's saved conversations (handy for "what did we decide
    about X"), and `list_models` lists the installed models and marks the
    loaded ones with their VRAM and unload time.
  - `system_status` answers resource questions: RAM, GPU VRAM and the
    model-disk space of the machine running Ollama, probed over ssh when
    the session uses `--remote` (`target="local"` reports this machine).
  - Read-only chemical-inventory lookups through `chemInventoryAPI`:
    `chem_search` (containers by name/CAS/barcode/GHS), `chem_container`
    (one full record with custom fields and GHS) and `chem_locations`
    (the location tree, or the containers in one location). The three
    tools only appear when the CLI is installed (`chem_inventory` names a
    different path, `chem_tools: false` hides them).

Version: 1.36

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Web search is more insistent about actually finding the answer. The
    `web_search` and `fetch_url` tool texts (now files, see below) say a
    search is a starting point, not a verdict: when the results do not clear
    the question up, search again with different or narrower words instead
    of concluding the information is not there. The no-results reply, the
    results footer and the 404/JavaScript-shell fetch notes carry the same
    nudge.
  - Computations go through code. The tool rules tell the model never to do
    arithmetic in its head — percentages, unit conversions, dates and large
    or precise numbers get a short `run_python` snippet, and the printed
    result is the answer — and the run_python definition says the same.
  - System prompts are actually sent now. `--system`/`--system-file`, config
    `system` and `/system` were stored, shown, exported and baked into
    `/save` models, but never reached `/api/chat`; the session prompt now
    leads every request as a system message (kept out of the history, so
    `/system` changes apply on the next turn). With nothing set, a small
    built-in default ships as `defs/prompts/system.md` (plus
    `system_tools.md`, the tool/web-search rules, when tools are on);
    `--no-system`, config `system: ""` or `/system off` send none, `/system
    clear` returns to the default, and `/system` alone shows what is in
    effect.
  - Model-facing text moved out of the code: one JSON per tool in
    `ollamarun_lib/defs/tools/` (name, description, JSON schema, safety
    flags, capability `requires`, aliases, handler), and the system, title
    and summarize prompts in `ollamarun_lib/defs/prompts/`. A same-named
    file under `~/etc/config_files/ollamarun-defs/` overrides the shipped
    one; bad files warn and are skipped instead of breaking the tool
    harness. Title and summarize now read their prompts from files too.

Version: 1.35

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The streaming footer carries more: the 5s windowed rate, its
    cumulative average, the streamed token count and a live context
    counter -- `~24.0 tok/s (avg 21.4) · 45 tok · ctx 12.345k/32.768k
    (38.123%)`. The ctx number is the last round's prompt + completion
    plus the chunks this round has already streamed, so it moves with
    every token (it starts on the second round, when there is a base to
    count from); the TUI status bar shows the same rate/average and live
    ctx. `/tps` and `--no-tps` still switch the whole thing off.
  - Context counters scale dynamically: raw below 1000 and 3-decimal k
    from 1k on (`845`, `12.345k`, `32.768k`), so a single token is
    visible while streaming, and the share reads with 3 decimals
    (`(38.123%)`). One shared formatter (`human_tokens`/
    `format_context`) feeds both front ends.

Version: 1.34

Summary of changes in this version:
  - `/num-ctx --max-cpu N [--probe]` fills DRAM instead of VRAM. It raises
    the context until the model's host RAM reaches N% (1-100) of the DRAM
    available to it: /proc/meminfo's MemAvailable on the ollama machine --
    one ssh probe for --remote hosts -- plus the RAM the loaded model already
    holds (/api/ps `size - size_vram`, back when it reloads; other programs
    and models keep theirs), less a max_cpu_overhead_mb allowance. Some
    layers stay on the GPU unless /gpu-layers is cpu -- a pinned layer count
    is honoured and only the host side counts toward the target; unpinned,
    the estimate simulates llama.cpp's fit (the weights and per-layer KV of
    the tail it can keep). `--probe` loads candidates, reads the real
    footprint back from /api/ps and solves the measured footprint line for
    the target (max_cpu_probes, default 8), never choosing a context above
    it, and leaves the model warm there. Recorded on a GTX 1050 + 32 GiB
    box: a fully CPU-placed qwen2.5:7b reported 5,203 MiB of host RAM in
    /api/ps while MemAvailable gave up 5,006 MiB, so the reported number
    tracks the machine's memory in steady state.
  - The CLI probe flag is now the mode-agnostic `--probe`: `--num-ctx
    max-gpu --probe` and `--num-ctx --max-cpu 80 --probe` (--max-gpu-probe
    stays as a hidden alias). The command line is `/num-ctx N | --max-gpu
    [--probe] | --max-cpu N [--probe]`.

Version: 1.33

Summary of changes in this version:
  - `--verbose` is obsolete: the `⏱ load … · prompt … · eval … · total …`
    timings line now prints under every answer by default on a terminal
    (REPL, TUI, one-shot, pager), as if the flag had been passed. Piped or
    redirected output stays clean unless `--verbose` is given explicitly --
    that is the flag's only remaining job, so `ollama run`-style command
    lines keep working unchanged. `--raw` and `--json` never print it.
  - Config `show_stats` is now an off-switch (default true): `false` silences
    the timings line, while an explicit `--verbose` still forces it on. The
    renderer's redundant `verbose` knob is gone; `resolve_show_stats()` in
    helpers.py owns the decision.

Version: 1.32

Summary of changes in this version:
  - The `speak` tool's result box now says where the voice comes from and
    where it will be heard: `tool · speak · ok · optiplex → g3 · 0.42s`.
    The new `tts-remote say --json` report names the machine that will
    synthesize (or the one that originally rendered a phrase served from
    the cache/saved file, marked `(cached)`) plus the machine that plays
    it back -- always the box ollamarun runs on, since playback happens
    there. Auto-speak (`--speak` / `/tts on`) prints the same `X → Y`
    after its char count.
  - The note rides on the session's tool message as `tool_note`, so
    `/show`, `--show` and the HTML export replay the titled box, and it
    stays display-only: what the model sees is unchanged. A tts-remote
    that predates `say --json` is re-run without the flag, so speech
    still queues (just without the note).

Version: 1.31

Summary of changes in this version:
  - `/num-ctx --max-gpu` no longer misreads a resident model as VRAM pressure.
    The free-VRAM reading is taken with the model already loaded, so its own
    bytes used to be counted twice -- once excluded from "free", then again
    deducted as weights -- and a 0.8B model on a 4 GiB card came back "the
    GPU placement does not fit" while it plainly did. Resident models are now
    credited back into the pool (their `/api/ps` size_vram is released when
    they reload at the new context; `max_gpu_credit_residents: false` limits
    the credit to the model being sized), so the report reads
    "1.8 GiB free + 2.6 GiB resident → 4.4 GiB pool", and when the per-device
    fit margin is the only blocker the estimate says so and prints the
    context it would fit without it.
  - `--probe` starts from that estimate instead of falling back to 4,096, and
    picks every next candidate by solving the measured bytes-per-token line
    for the capacity (llama.cpp's footprint is affine in num_ctx) instead of
    climbing bisection midpoints, so a good estimate needs one or two probes
    and the default budget of 8 brackets the boundary instead of walking
    toward it a step at a time.

Version: 1.30

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Sortable model pickers. `--sort name|size|parameters|context|modified`
    (default `modified`, newest first) orders the picker that `ollamarun`
    opens without a model, and the picker prompt itself accepts `--sort KEY`
    (also `-s KEY`/`sort KEY`) to re-order the list in place -- numbers,
    names and `q` keep working, and a sort chosen in the picker sticks for
    the rest of the session. `/model` (now also `/models`) takes
    `--sort KEY` and `--list --sort KEY`; numeric sorts list the largest
    first and models missing a value sort last. `context` needs one
    `/api/show` per model, fetched lazily behind a spinner and cached per
    host; the table gains `params` and (once known) `ctx` columns, and Tab
    completes `--list`/`--sort` plus the sort keys.

Version: 1.29

Summary of changes in this version:
  - New TUI front end: `--ui tui` (config key `"ui"`) replaces the scrolling
    prompt with three panes -- the transcript, a rule, and a status bar above
    an input box that is always available, even while the model is working.
    The transcript streams the same markdown/2D-math rendering as before,
    keeps thinking collapsed (Ctrl+T), shows tool calls and results in order,
    and scrolls with the mouse/PgUp. Textual is optional: `--ui inline` (the
    default, unchanged prompt_toolkit REPL) and `--ui auto` pick the TUI only
    when the `textual` package (1.0+) is installed, and `--ui tui` degrades to
    the inline REPL with a note when it is not.
  - Status bar: spinner + state (ready, generating, running, tool, approval,
    stopping, done, error), the model (and `--remote` machine), the
    thinking/tools/render modes, context tokens and percentage, live tok/s,
    the queued count and the key hints. It trims itself to the terminal width,
    dropping the modes first.
  - Type while the model works. Enter queues: messages are handed to the model
    at the next sub-task boundary -- right before the following /api/chat of
    the same turn, i.e. immediately after a tool result -- so it keeps working
    with the new information instead of dropping what it was doing. Slash
    commands that only read state or change display knobs (`/thinking`, /tps,
    /render, /stats, /context, /queue, /ps, /show, /tools, /tts, /help, /params,
    /tag) run straight away; everything else queues and runs when the turn
    ends, in the order it was typed (`/queue [list|clear|rm N]` shows or edits
    the list).
  - Ctrl+B sends what is typed right now: it cuts a streaming answer short but
    never kills a tool that is already running -- a shell/python command keeps
    running in the background and the model gets its result, together with your
    message, in the next round.
  - Esc Esc stops the turn (the first Esc arms it and the status bar says
    "press Esc again to stop"). The partial answer is kept and marked cancelled
    -- exactly like Ctrl+C today -- and anything queued then runs as the next
    turn, so nothing typed is lost. Ctrl+Q quits (a second press when the model
    is busy or messages are queued), Ctrl+L clears the transcript.
  - Cancellation plumbing underneath: a `CancelToken` is checked between stream
    chunks and `OllamaClient.abort_stream()` closes the in-flight response from
    the UI thread, so a stop lands even while the stream is blocked waiting for
    the model to produce its next token (a long model load, for instance).
  - Tool approvals ask in a modal that offers the same numbered choices as the
    console prompt (`1/y` run once, `2/a` always allow that exact call, one
    line per suggested generalized rule, `A` auto-allow every later call,
    `n` deny) -- type the number or letter, or move with up/down and press
    enter. The choice list is built in `ui.py` so the console prompt and the
    modal share one implementation, and the command plus the reason stay in
    front of you. The model/session pickers, the pull confirmation and the
    `$EDITOR` handoff keep working too (the pickers ask inside the app, the
    editor suspends it).
  - Tests: the queue/boundary rules, cancel plumbing, status-line layout and
    the whole TUI (Textual's own headless driver: typing, queueing, stopping,
    Ctrl+B, the approval prompt, quitting) are covered in test_ollamarun.py;
    they skip cleanly when Textual is missing.

Version: 1.28

Summary of changes in this version:
  - Every tool panel now wears the tool's colour. The result box joins the
    call panel -- it used to keep a green/red ok/failed border -- so both
    boxes are bordered in the tool's colour and the ok/failed state moved
    into the title ("tool · run_shell · ok · 0.12s", green ok / red failed).
    The approval prompt prints "NAME needs confirmation" with the tool name
    in its colour, and the `/tools` status table lists each tool in its
    colour too.
  - Approval rules. The tool-confirmation prompt lists numbered choices and
    you type one: `1/y` runs once, `2/a` always allows that exact call, the
    middle numbers always allow a suggested generalized rule, `A`
    auto-allows every later call and `n` denies. The suggested rules are
    session-scoped and derived from the call itself -- `shell: mkdir -p :*`
    (a literal command prefix), `path: ~/proj/**` (a directory tree,
    writes included) and `python: imports os,sys` (module roots a snippet
    may import and use).
  - Stored rules are re-checked on every later call, unattended runs
    included, and each row names exactly what it would allow. Shell rules
    match a literal prefix plus a `:*` tail and never cover compound
    commands; path rules resolve symlinks and cannot escape the directory
    they were created in; python rules never waive `open`/`.system`-style
    calls, wildcard imports or anything outside their module set.
  - A hard floor overrules every rule: substitutions, redirections, pipes
    into an interpreter, wrappers (sudo/ssh/env/xargs/...), interpreters,
    disk/power tools and `rm -rf /`, `~` or `*` always ask again.
  - `/tools rules` lists every stored grant (exact fingerprints included),
    `rm N…` removes one and `add shell|path|python|call …` adds one by hand;
    the `/tools` status line shows the count.
  - Context fitting for the GPU. `/num-ctx --max-gpu` (CLI: `--num-ctx
    max-gpu`) picks the largest context that still keeps the model fully on
    the GPU. The estimate is built from what is deterministic: KV-cache
    bytes per token from /api/show's GGUF metadata (llama.cpp's own formula,
    per-layer head_count_kv arrays from hybrid models included,
    key/value_length fallbacks, OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL slices and
    OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE element sizes), the on-disk model size from
    /api/tags, and the machine's free VRAM -- nvidia-smi locally, one
    BatchMode ssh probe for --remote hosts (reading LLAMA_ARG_FIT_TARGET /
    OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL / OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE from the ollama unit too).
    A pinned /gpu-layers is honoured (the fit targets that layer set, and
    with per-layer KV metadata only the attention blocks in the offloaded
    tail count). The fit margin mirrors llama.cpp's 1 GiB per device
    (config max_gpu_margin_mb) and max_gpu_overhead_mb reserves room for the
    compute/output/recurrent buffers the metadata cannot see. The chosen
    value is set like any other /num-ctx value; a machine whose ollama is
    bound to its own tailnet address is recognised as "this machine".
  - `/num-ctx --max-gpu --probe` (CLI: `--max-gpu-probe`) verifies by
    loading: the model is warmed at candidate contexts and each real GPU
    split is read back from /api/ps. Partial loads only contribute their
    sign (their reported size double-counts CPU buffers), so candidates are
    re-placed by bisecting footprint bytes -- the size a full offload would
    report is affine in the context, exactly the relation llama.cpp's own
    fit interpolates -- until the largest 100%-GPU context is bracketed
    (config max_gpu_probes, default 8). Probe loads unload first so the
    free-VRAM reading is clean, and the model is left warm at the chosen
    context. `/num-ctx --help` documents the whole thing.
  - `host_is_self()` (helpers) recognises a base URL that points at this
    machine by hostname, its resolved addresses or its own tailnet IPs, so
    `--host <my-tailscale-ip>` gets a local VRAM probe and `--remote` on the
    machine you are already on falls back to local instead of a failing ssh.
  - Tool calls are colour-coded. Every tool has its own colour for the call
    panel (the arguments box after the model asks for a tool), the
    "running NAME…" line and the "tool · NAME" title of its result panel --
    the result border still says ok (green) / failed (red) -- so a mixed
    run of run_shell/read_file/fetch_url/... calls is readable at a glance,
    including in `/show`, `--show` and HTML exports. A tool without a fixed
    colour (an invented name, a future tool) picks one at random from a
    palette, remembered for the rest of the run so all its panels match,
    and config `tool_colors` (tool name -> rich colour, e.g.
    {"run_shell": "red"}) overrides any of them.
  - Tool-loop separation. A tool loop ends when the model finally commits to
    an answer (a round that finishes without requesting more tools); that
    answer is now separated from the processing above it by a bright
    centered `response` rule, so narration that precedes further tool calls
    is no longer mistaken for the answer. A round's fate is only known once
    it finishes, so nothing changes while it streams -- the rule lands in
    the frozen frame the moment the answer's round commits. Turns that never
    call a tool render exactly as before, and cancelled/errored rounds are
    left unmarked.
  - Smarter web tooling. New `web_search` tool (present when the `ddgs`
    package is installed): it returns titles, URLs and snippets, so the
    model stops guessing URLs, and its results are cached per session.
    `fetch_url` now returns readable page text instead of raw markup that
    was truncated to a few KB of <head>/<script>: HTML becomes plain text
    plus the page's links (the <article>/<main> region wins), JSON is
    pretty-printed, and PDFs are text-extracted via pypdf or pdftotext.
  - Failed fetches now teach instead of failing silently: 404/410, 403
    bot walls (Cloudflare/Akamai), 429, 5xx, timeouts and DNS errors come
    back as one actionable line ("the page does not exist... call
    web_search") instead of the site's error page, JavaScript-only shells
    and empty bodies say so explicitly, a search-engine URL (a Google/
    Bing/DuckDuckGo /search page) is rerouted to `web_search`, and a URL
    already fetched in the session is answered from that cache.
  - The tool dispatcher resolves invented tool names onto the real ones
    (`search`/`google` -> `web_search`, `browse`/`curl`/`get_url` ->
    `fetch_url`) and tells the model the real name; both web tools are
    read-only, so like the other read-only tools they never prompt.
  - Ctrl+T no longer opens the thinking in the system pager (`less`): it now
    expands the boxed thinking panel directly in the live view -- the same
    panel you used to land on only after quitting the pager -- and pressing
    it again collapses it. The stream never pauses. `/thinking full` prints
    the stored thinking panel straight to the terminal for the same reason.
  - Table rendering. Inline code spans (and code fences) no longer split the
    response into separate markdown blocks: any table, list or paragraph
    that contained `code` used to be chopped apart at every span, so a
    table whose cells held backticked commands rendered as one wrapped
    paragraph of pipes. Text now stays whole, and a `$$...$$`/`\\[...\\]`
    block is extracted only when it sits on its own line(s). Display math
    written mid-line -- inside a table cell, a list item or a sentence --
    now renders inline, so it cannot break the block around it.
  - Also fixed the version drift from the previous release: `VERSION` still
    read 1.18 while the docstring had moved to 1.19.
  - tts-remote moved to a git submodule package (`~/bin/tts-remote/`); its
    `find_tts_remote` fallback now understands the new layout
    (`~/bin/tts-remote/tts-remote`), so the speak tool and auto-speak keep
    working even when that directory is not on PATH.
  - GPU layers. `--gpu-layers SPEC` and `/gpu-layers [SPEC]` control how
    many model layers run on the GPU: `20` (absolute), `50%` (relative to
    the model's layer count from /api/show, so 100% is a full offload),
    `+4`/`-4` (adjust the current value; with nothing set, "all except the
    last N" against a full offload), `all`, `cpu` or `auto`. The spec is
    stored in the session and re-resolved per model, wins over a
    `/set parameter num_gpu`, unloads a resident model on change so the
    next request reloads with the new placement, and `/params` shows the
    resolved value.
  - Missing models now offer to pull. Starting (or switching to) a model
    that is not installed on the target machine -- local or `--remote` --
    prints that machine's free disk space and asks to pull it with live
    per-layer progress. Declining opens the model picker for the machine.
    `--pull` skips the question (one-shots/scripts), `/pull [MODEL]` pulls
    or updates a model from the REPL, and non-interactive runs without
    `--pull` fail with a clear hint. Disk space comes from
    shutil.disk_usage locally and a BatchMode ssh `df` probe (reading
    OLLAMA_MODELS from the ollama unit) for `--remote` machines; when ssh
    is unavailable it degrades to "unknown".
  - Remote machines. `--remote NAME` runs the model on a tailnet box --
    pi, kiddo, g3, optiplex7070 (alias optiplex) or guri, plus any
    host[:port] -- resolving the name through `tailscale status --json`
    and pointing every call at that machine's API. Models load, generate
    and unload there (background preload, /api/ps, /model and /load,
    REPL exit), while sessions, files, images and the tool harness stay
    local exactly as before. A short /api/version preflight fails fast
    with the tailscale node state when the machine is unreachable.
  - Resuming a session reconnects to the machine it last ran on (the
    session records both the resolved base and the `--remote` name), so
    `ollamarun -c` continues on e.g. kiddo without flags; the banner
    shows the remote name next to the host. --host/--remote override it.
  - Config keys `remote` (default machine, like `host` for the local
    server) and `remote_hosts` (extra or overridden name -> host[:port]).
  - tts-remote integration. The speak tool queues text through `tts-remote say`
    (text piped on stdin, fire-and-forget, never blocks the answer) and
    list_voices exposes the installed voice catalog, so the model can read
    things aloud or pick a voice/language on request.
  - Auto-speak: `--speak` (or config `tts_auto`, or `/tts on`) reads every
    final answer aloud through `tts-remote say --markdown`; only the final
    answer is spoken (not thinking or tool rounds). `--no-speak` overrides a
    session that remembered it.
  - New `/tts` command: status, `on|off`, `voice NAME`, `lang CODE`,
    `tools on|off` (whether the model may call speak/list_voices) and `voices`.
    Voice/language/auto-speak are stored in the session like other settings.
    Config keys: tts_auto, tts_voice, tts_lang, tts_remote, tts_tools.
  - Tool harness. The model can now call tools through Ollama's function-calling
    API and the results are fed back for a final answer (up to 8 rounds, each
    streamed and rendered as before). Built-in tools: run_shell, run_python,
    read_file, write_file, list_dir and fetch_url, plus tts-remote's speak and
    list_voices when it is installed -- all built in ollamarun_lib/tools.py.
  - Allowlist-based approvals. Read-only tools (read_file/list_dir/fetch_url)
    always run; run_shell runs unattended only when every command segment is on
    the safe list (ls, cat, grep, rg, find without -exec/-delete, read-only git,
    ...) and run_python only when an AST scan proves the snippet cannot touch
    files or the OS; everything else asks through the numbered prompt, which
    can remember that exact call or store a broader rule. Unattended runs deny
    non-approved calls with a note the
    model can explain, unless -y/--yes or `/tools policy auto`; write_file
    always asks by default.
  - Tool calls and results are stored in the session (assistant tool_calls plus
    role:tool messages carrying tool_name) and replayed on later turns; exports,
    --show and /undo understand them. --format switches tools off (the two
    cannot be combined) and a model that does not advertise tool support gets a
    warning while the schemas are still sent.
  - New flags and commands: --tools/--no-tools, -y/--yes now also skips tool
    confirmations, and /tools [on|off|policy auto|allowlist|ask|enable NAME|
    disable NAME|allow|rules]. Config keys: tools, tools_policy,
    tools_shell_allow, tools_timeout, tools_max_output, tools_max_rounds.
  - Fixed --raw: it now actually switches the renderer to raw mode (it was
    accepted but ignored; use --render raw as before too).
  - Fixed context-window accounting: `/context`, the "context nearly full"
    warning and auto-compact used only `last_prompt_tokens` (the
    `prompt_eval_count` of the request that produced the last answer), which
    excludes that answer even though it is re-sent on the next turn. They now
    count prompt + completion tokens (the tokens the next request will carry),
    so usage no longer under-reports by the length of the last reply. `cmd_stats`
    gained `last completion`/`last context` rows, and `/summarize` resets both.
  - Rewrote the startup "waiting for model" indicator again: the previous
    two attempts (a rich Live spinner that ended up blocking the prompt,
    then a prompt_toolkit bottom_toolbar) both turned out broken in a real
    terminal -- the toolbar anchors to the terminal's last row, leaving a
    large blank gap and never appearing near the prompt. It's now the
    prompt's `rprompt` (right-aligned, same line as the input), animated via
    the session's refresh_interval: visible immediately, updates in place,
    doesn't block typing, and disappears once the model is resident. The
    background thread's `ollama ps` print is now safely interleaved with the
    active prompt via prompt_toolkit's own `patch_stdout()` helper.
  - Fixed a regression from the previous version, which had accidentally
    made the startup model preload block the prompt from appearing until
    loading finished. It's non-blocking again: the "waiting for MODEL…"
    spinner now runs in the background and clears itself once the prompt
    is ready to accept input, without waiting for the model to finish
    loading. Only sending an actual message still waits on that load.
  - The "loading MODEL…" line the background preload printed at session
    start is now the same animated dots spinner already used while waiting
    for a response mid-stream ("waiting for MODEL…"), instead of a plain
    static line -- reusing the existing widget rather than a one-off.
  - The background model preload now prints a "loading MODEL…" line as soon
    as the session starts, so there's a visible sign it's happening instead
    of the model silently warming up with no feedback. If a message is sent
    before that preload finishes, sending it now waits for that same load
    (instead of letting `/api/chat` kick off a second, redundant one), so
    `ollama ps` shows up right when the model becomes resident instead of
    only after the whole first response has finished generating.
  - Fixed the model's response being printed twice at the end of a stream:
    the live view's last frame could go stale (throttled refreshes may skip
    the final chunk), and the code used to paper over that by printing the
    whole rule + response a second time below it. It now forces one last
    live refresh before the live view freezes, so nothing is rendered twice.
  - `ollama ps` now prints as soon as the background-preloaded model finishes
    loading (typically while you're still typing the first message), instead
    of waiting until after the first response finished generating.
  - Model turns are now marked with a `rich` rule/divider instead of a full
    box, and the user/model chat colors are softer, muted pastel tones
    (was bold cyan/green) instead of the previous saturated colors.
  - The REPL now starts loading the session's model in the background as
    soon as the session opens, instead of waiting for the first message --
    it's already warming up while the user types. Pressing Enter on an
    empty line now sends immediately when there are pending images (e.g.
    from `/image PATH`), instead of being silently ignored, so attaching
    an image and hitting Enter with no text sends it for the model to
    process right away.
  - `/image` now parses its argument with shlex, so a quoted path
    (`/image "my photo.png"`) with spaces attaches correctly instead of
    being split into multiple bogus paths at each whitespace.
  - Split into a package. The implementation now lives in ollamarun_lib/
    (one module per pipeline stage: constants, helpers, mathrender, files,
    images, renderer, client, session, emit, interactive, repl, run, cli --
    see CLAUDE.md's Architecture section and each module's docstring) instead
    of one ~4300-line file. This script is now a thin entry point that adds
    its own directory to sys.path and calls ollamarun_lib.cli.main(). No
    behavior change; `ollamarun_lib` also re-exports every public name at
    the top level (`import ollamarun_lib as orun`) so existing callers and
    tests keep working unmodified.
  - Chat bubbles. User turns render as a small right-aligned box; model
    answers render inside a full-width box titled with the model name --
    live while streaming and in `/show`/`--show` transcript replays. Plain
    and raw render modes, and non-terminal output, are unaffected.
  - `/ps` shows the live `ollama ps` table (model, size, VRAM, quant,
    until-idle). The REPL also prints it automatically the first time a
    model finishes loading in a session.
  - `--num-ctx N` (CLI) and `/num-ctx [N]` (REPL) set the context window
    size. A request above the model's max context (read from `/api/show`)
    is capped to that max with a warning instead of being sent as-is.
  - 2D math layout. Display math ($$...$$, \\[...\\]) is now drawn as a real
    Unicode block -- stacked fractions with a bar, sqrt overlines, sum/prod/
    lim limits above-below, integral limits on the side, matrices, cases,
    aligned columns, \\boxed borders, \\binom, tall delimiters, accents and
    \\mathbb/\\mathcal/\\mathfrak/\\mathbf letters -- centered in cyan and
    independent of the markdown flow. Inline math is flattened to a single
    line with proper operator spacing. Anything unsupported (unknown
    environment, over-wide, over-budget) falls back to the old pylatexenc
    conversion; the parser never raises.
  - Model housekeeping. Resident models are looked up via /api/ps and unloaded
    with keep_alive=0: `/model` and `/load --model` unload the current model
    before switching, `/load SESSION` asks "load with current model or last
    model?" and unloads when the session's model wins, and leaving the REPL
    (/bye, Ctrl+D) unloads too. One-shot runs stay warm unless `--unload`;
    `--keep-loaded` disables the exit unload. Embedding models are never
    unloaded. Ctrl+C while a command prompt is open no longer kills the REPL.
  - Live tokens/s estimator. A `~42.3 tok/s · 137 tok` footer streams under
    the answer (thinking + answer chunks, 5s sliding window), with `--no-tps`,
    config `tps_footer` and `/tps on|off`.
  - Thinking is rendered like the answer (dim markdown + 2D math) and is
    scrollable: Ctrl+T (or `/thinking full`) opens the full accumulated
    thinking in the pager, then the stream resumes; an expanded preview is
    tail-capped to the terminal height and the full text is printed into
    scrollback when the answer starts.
  - Thinking renders live. The streaming view redraws on `message.thinking`
    chunks too (previously only answer chunks triggered a Live update, so a
    long chain-of-thought ran behind a blank screen), and it opens with a
    "waiting for model…" spinner so cold model loads show activity.
  - Collapsible thinking display. Default is collapsed: an animated
    "thinking · 1.2k chars · Ctrl+T to expand" spinner line while the model
    reasons, then a one-line footer above the answer. Ctrl+T expands or
    collapses mid-stream (expanded mode shows a rolling tail preview; the
    full text is kept in the session and in `--show`/exports). `--show-thinking`
    starts expanded, `--hidethinking` starts hidden, config key `thinking`.
  - New `/thinking [show|collapse|hide|toggle|full]` REPL command and the
    Ctrl+T shortcut documented in `/? shortcuts`.
  - Initial version. Drop-in superset of `ollama run MODEL [PROMPT]`: every
    ollama 0.34 flag is accepted (--dimensions, --format, --hidethinking,
    --insecure, --keepalive, --nowordwrap, --think, --truncate, --verbose),
    plus --host/-m/--system/--option/--image and session management flags
    (--continue, --resume, --session, --list, --show, --search, --export,
    --delete, --reindex) and --json/--raw scripting output.
  - Markdown rendering with Live incremental re-render while streaming;
    LaTeX math ($...$, $$...$$, \\(...\\), \\[...\\]) is converted to Unicode
    via pylatexenc (with an inline ^/_ super/subscript pass) and protected
    from code fences/spans. --render markdown|plain|raw, --no-math,
    --nowordwrap.
  - Every conversation is saved to ~/etc/data/ollamarun (one atomic JSON per
    session plus index.json): resume/continue/view/search/export (md, json,
    html) /tag/delete (via trash-put). Prompt input history is kept with
    prompt_toolkit unless OLLAMA_NOHISTORY is set.
  - Interactive REPL on prompt_toolkit: /help, /set (parameter|system),
    /show, /load, /save (creates a real Ollama model via /api/create),
    /clear, /bye, /? shortcuts -- plus /undo, /retry, /edit, /branch,
    /context, /summarize, /sessions, /title, /tag, /model, /params, /think,
    /system, /image, /render, /math, /pager, /copy, /export, /stats, /new.
    Tab completion for commands, session ids, model names and @file paths;
    Ctrl+G opens $OLLAMA_EDITOR/$EDITOR; Ctrl+C cancels a generation without
    leaving the session.
  - Vision support (--image PATH / /image PATH, persisted with the session),
    @path file mentions inlined into the prompt with a size guard, rich
    pager, /copy via pyperclip, auto titles, tags and context-window
    accounting with optional /summarize compaction.
  - Raw HTTP client (requests) so all traffic can go through llm-jukebox on
    :11434; embedding-capability models with --dimensions/--truncate route
    to /api/embed and print a vector summary (full vector with --json).

Usage:
    ollamarun MODEL [PROMPT]                 # one-shot or interactive REPL
    ollamarun --remote kiddo MODEL [PROMPT]  # run on a tailnet machine
    ollamarun                                # pick a model, start a session
    ollamarun -c                             # continue the most recent session
    ollamarun -c SESSION_ID                  # continue a specific session
    ollamarun -r                             # browse and resume sessions
    ollamarun --list                         # list saved sessions
    ollamarun --show SESSION_ID              # render a saved transcript
    ollamarun --search TEXT                  # search across sessions
    ollamarun --export SESSION_ID --export-format md --out FILE
    ollamarun --delete SESSION_ID [-y]       # move session to trash

Config: ~/etc/config_files/ollamarun.json (created with defaults on first
run; `remote` picks a default machine, `remote_hosts` adds names). Sessions:
~/etc/data/ollamarun/sessions/<id>.json + index.json. Prompt history:
~/etc/data/ollamarun/history. Debug log: ~/etc/logs/ollamarun.log
(with --debug).

Implementation: ollamarun_lib/ (see CLAUDE.md's Architecture section).
"""
